ze-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#use-the-object-form-for-targeted-remaps","content":"`byFileName` for exact basenames such as `package.json`"},"27":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#17","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#use-the-object-form-for-targeted-remaps","content":"`byFileExtension` for suffixes such as `ts` or `spec.ts`"},"28":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#18","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#use-the-object-form-for-targeted-remaps","content":"`byFileNameContains` for broader patterns such as `dockerfile`"},"29":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#19","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#use-the-object-form-for-targeted-remaps","content":"This approach keeps the built-in mapping for every icon that you did not change."},"30":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#20","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#rule-precedence-matters","content":"File-specific rules win over broader rules. `solid-trees` resolves icons in this\norder:"},"31":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#21","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#rule-precedence-matters","content":"exact basename matches"},"32":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#22","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#rule-precedence-matters","content":"basename-contains matches"},"33":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#23","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#rule-precedence-matters","content":"extension matches, and the more specific suffix wins"},"34":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#24","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#rule-precedence-matters","content":"the chosen built-in set"},"35":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#25","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#rule-precedence-matters","content":"the generic file-slot remap or fallback"},"36":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#26","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#rule-precedence-matters","content":"For the full lookup contract, read\nIcons."},"37":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#27","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#use-a-sprite-sheet-only-for-advanced-cases","content":"Use `spriteSheet` in two cases. Use it when you already have branded SVG\nsymbols. Also use it when you need a few custom symbols next to the built-in\nset."},"38":{"id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#28","page_id":"/docs/guides/customize-icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/customize-icons#use-a-sprite-sheet-only-for-advanced-cases","content":"Keep the contract simple. Provide `<symbol>` definitions. Then point remap rules\nat those symbols. Each tree prefixes the symbol ids of its sprite sheet, so\nseveral trees in one document never resolve each other's icons. Do not make\nsprite sheets the default docs path."},"39":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","type":"page","content":"Get started","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/get-started"},"40":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#0","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started","content":"Create one tree model with createFileTree and render it with the FileTree component."},"41":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#1","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/get-started#path-first-identity","content":"Path-first identity"},"42":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#2","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/get-started#install-solid-trees","content":"Install `solid-trees`"},"43":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#3","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/get-started#create-the-model-with-createfiletree","content":"Create the model with `createFileTree(...)`"},"44":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#4","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/get-started#render-with-filetree-modelmodel-","content":"Render with `<FileTree model={model} />`"},"45":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#5","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/get-started#read-and-update-tree-state-through-the-model","content":"Read and update tree state through the model"},"46":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#6","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/get-started#use-simple-paths-input-only-when-the-tree-is-small","content":"Use simple `paths` input only when the tree is small"},"47":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#7","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/get-started#move-to-prepared-input-before-the-tree-gets-expensive","content":"Move to prepared input before the tree gets expensive"},"48":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#8","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started","content":"`solid-trees` gives you one path-first model and a thin Solid layer over it:\n`createFileTree(...)` creates the model, and `<FileTree model={...} />` renders\nit. The primitives `createFileTreeSelection(...)`, `createFileTreeSearch(...)`,\nand `createFileTreeSelector(...)` derive reactive values from the same model."},"49":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#9","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#path-first-identity","content":"Use canonical path strings as the public identity for each item in the tree. For\nexample, `src/components/Button.tsx` is more than a label on screen. It is the\nvalue that you read from selection state. It is the path that you focus in code.\nIt is also the target that you rename or move later."},"50":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#10","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#path-first-identity","content":"To learn the shared terms behind this rule, read\nShared concepts."},"51":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#11","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#install-solid-trees","content":"The package needs Solid 2 (`solid-js` and `@solidjs/web` `^2.0.3`) next to it."},"52":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#12","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#install-solid-trees","content":"Import the stylesheet once. `<FileTree>` injects the structural layer itself, so\ntrees work without it, but the stylesheet adds the visual styles."},"53":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#13","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#create-the-model-with-createfiletree","content":"`createFileTree(...)` creates the model one time. The current reactive owner\nowns it, so the model is cleaned up when the owner disposes. Options are read\nonce. Later option changes do not update the model. Update the model through\nmethods when the tree data or runtime behavior changes after mount. The methods\ninclude `resetPaths(...)`, `setComposition(...)`, `setGitStatus(...)`, and\n`setIcons(...)`."},"54":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#14","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#create-the-model-with-createfiletree","content":"For small trees, pass raw `paths`. For scalable trees, use `preparedInput`.\nCreate `preparedInput` on the server or another non-UI boundary."},"55":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#15","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#create-the-model-with-createfiletree","content":"Read\nShape tree data for fast rendering\nafter this quickstart if you must still decide how to shape that input."},"56":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#16","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#render-with-filetree-modelmodel-","content":"`<FileTree />` is a thin Solid view over the model. It renders the tree into the\nlight DOM. It forwards normal host props such as `class` and `style`. The tree\nvirtualizes its rows, so give it a bounded height. Swapping the `model` prop\nremounts the view."},"57":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#17","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#render-with-filetree-modelmodel-","content":"Keep the mental model simple. The model owns the tree state. The Solid component\nrenders it."},"58":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#18","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#read-and-update-tree-state-through-the-model","content":"Solid code reads from the model through primitives and model getters. Solid code\nwrites back through model methods."},"59":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#19","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#read-and-update-tree-state-through-the-model","content":"Use `createFileTreeSelector(model, selector, isEqual?)` when sibling UI needs a\ncustom derived value. It only notifies when the selected value changes. Model\ngetters such as `getFocusedPath()` also track inside Solid scopes, so you can\nread them directly in JSX. For the shared interaction terms, read\nNavigate selection, focus, and search\nand Solid API."},"60":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#20","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#use-simple-paths-input-only-when-the-tree-is-small","content":"Raw `paths` is the low-ceremony path for demos, tests, and very small static\ntrees. It is not the scalable default. Move the shape or sort work out of the UI\nwhen the tree becomes expensive on the client."},"61":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#21","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#move-to-prepared-input-before-the-tree-gets-expensive","content":"Use this recommended scale path:"},"62":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#22","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#move-to-prepared-input-before-the-tree-gets-expensive","content":"Load canonical paths on the server or another non-UI boundary."},"63":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#23","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#move-to-prepared-input-before-the-tree-gets-expensive","content":"Prepare the tree input one time."},"64":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#24","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#move-to-prepared-input-before-the-tree-gets-expensive","content":"Pass `preparedInput` into `createFileTree(...)`."},"65":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#25","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#move-to-prepared-input-before-the-tree-gets-expensive","content":"Use `preparePresortedFileTreeInput(...)` when the server already knows the final\norder. It is the highest-performance prepared-input variant. The client can skip\nboth the shaping work and the extra sorting work."},"66":{"id":"/docs/guides/get-started#26","page_id":"/docs/guides/get-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/get-started#move-to-prepared-input-before-the-tree-gets-expensive","content":"Unlike upstream, `solid-trees` renders on the client only. There is no SSR\npreload or hydration step. See\nDifferences from `@pierre/trees`."},"67":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","type":"page","content":"Handle large trees efficiently","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently"},"68":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#0","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","content":"Shape and order the data before it reaches the UI, then tune rendering only where it helps."},"69":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#1","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#start-with-the-main-recommendation","content":"Start with the main recommendation"},"70":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#2","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#prepare-input-before-it-reaches-the-client","content":"Prepare input before it reaches the client"},"71":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#3","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"Keep rendering work small"},"72":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#4","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#expansion-and-search-still-affect-scale","content":"Expansion and search still affect scale"},"73":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#5","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#swap-data-in-place","content":"Swap data in place"},"74":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#6","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#common-pitfalls","content":"Common pitfalls"},"75":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#7","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#what-to-read-next","content":"What to read next"},"76":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#8","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","content":"To make large trees fast, first reduce unnecessary client work. Shape and order\nthe data before it reaches the UI. Then tune rendering only where the visible\nwindow needs help."},"77":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#9","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#start-with-the-main-recommendation","content":"Use:"},"78":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#10","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#start-with-the-main-recommendation","content":"raw `paths` for small demos and low-ceremony cases"},"79":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#11","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#start-with-the-main-recommendation","content":"`preparedInput` for larger trees"},"80":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#12","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#start-with-the-main-recommendation","content":"`preparePresortedFileTreeInput(...)` when the server already owns the final\norder"},"81":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#13","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#start-with-the-main-recommendation","content":"This order matters. Do not go straight to the rendering knobs while the client\nstill does avoidable shape or sort work."},"82":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#14","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#prepare-input-before-it-reaches-the-client","content":"For larger trees, move the shape work out of the UI. Prepared input is the\nscale-oriented public path."},"83":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#15","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#prepare-input-before-it-reaches-the-client","content":"Use `prepareFileTreeInput(...)` instead when you have only an unsorted path\nlist. In both cases, pass the prepared result into `createFileTree(...)` or\n`resetPaths(...)`."},"84":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#16","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"`solid-trees` already virtualizes the visible row window. Most apps do not need\ncustom virtualization primitives."},"85":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#17","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"The main rendering knobs are:"},"86":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#18","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"the host element CSS height, because layout sets the steady-state viewport\nsize"},"87":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#19","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"`initialVisibleRowCount`, to budget first-render work before measurement"},"88":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#20","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"`density`, when your design changes the row density enough to matter. Pass\n`'compact' | 'default' | 'relaxed'` or a custom numeric factor. This option\nsets both the row height and the spacing in one place. Use `itemHeight` only\nwhen you need a row height that does not match a preset."},"89":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#21","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"`overscan`, to trade a little extra work for smoother scrolling"},"90":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#22","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"Give the rendered tree host a real CSS height. The row-count hint only shapes\nthe first render. The browser then measures the actual viewport."},"91":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#23","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#keep-rendering-work-small","content":"Keep the language outcome-focused. The tree mounts the visible slice and a small\nbuffer around it."},"92":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#24","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#expansion-and-search-still-affect-scale","content":"Prepared input lowers the cost to shape the data. But expansion and search still\nchange how many rows stay visible at once. Large expanded trees and broad search\nresults can widen the mounted window."},"93":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#25","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#expansion-and-search-still-affect-scale","content":"This is another reason to start with the input story first. Do not recompute the\ntree shape in the client while the visible tree also changes often."},"94":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#26","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#swap-data-in-place","content":"When the path list changes wholesale, keep the model and call `resetPaths(...)`.\nPass `{ preparedInput }` alone to skip the parse and sort that raw `paths` would\nrequire. Swapping the `model` prop of `<FileTree>` remounts the whole view."},"95":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#27","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#common-pitfalls","content":"Avoid these patterns when the tree grows:"},"96":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#28","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#common-pitfalls","content":"you send only raw `paths` for huge server-known datasets"},"97":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#29","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#common-pitfalls","content":"you re-sort on the client after the server already chose the order"},"98":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#30","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#common-pitfalls","content":"you use low-level virtualization ideas as the first fix"},"99":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#31","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#common-pitfalls","content":"you rebuild the model when `resetPaths(...)` with matching prepared input does\nthe job"},"100":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#32","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#what-to-read-next","content":"Shape tree data for fast rendering\nfor the shared input story"},"101":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#33","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#what-to-read-next","content":"Shared concepts\nfor the rendering knobs"},"102":{"id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#34","page_id":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/handle-large-trees-efficiently#what-to-read-next","content":"Model API for `resetPaths(...)` and the other\ndata methods"},"103":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","type":"page","content":"Navigate selection, focus, and search","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search"},"104":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#0","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","content":"How selection, focus, keyboard movement, and search share one path-first model."},"105":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#1","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#start-with-the-interaction-state-model","content":"Start with the interaction state model"},"106":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#2","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#selection-and-focus-are-related-but-different","content":"Selection and focus are related but different"},"107":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#3","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#keyboard-navigation-follows-the-visible-tree","content":"Keyboard navigation follows the visible tree"},"108":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#4","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#add-search-on-top-of-that-same-state","content":"Add search on top of that same state"},"109":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#5","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#guide-default-hide-non-matches","content":"Guide default: `hide-non-matches`"},"110":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#6","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#solid-integration-notes","content":"Solid integration notes"},"111":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#7","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#what-to-read-next","content":"What to read next"},"112":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#8","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","content":"The tree model tracks three user-facing states: selection, focus, and the\nvisible row set. Search builds on the same model. It does not add a separate\nidentity system."},"113":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#9","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#start-with-the-interaction-state-model","content":"Selection, focus, keyboard movement, and search all use canonical paths. The\nvisible tree can change when a branch collapses or when search removes rows.\nYour app still uses the same path values."},"114":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#10","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#start-with-the-interaction-state-model","content":"For this reason, the public APIs return path-based results. Selected paths are a\n`readonly string[]`. Focused items are path strings. Search matches are path\nstrings."},"115":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#11","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#selection-and-focus-are-related-but-different","content":"Focus tells you where keyboard actions land. Selection tells you which rows the\napp treats as chosen. The two often move together. But they are not the same."},"116":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#12","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#selection-and-focus-are-related-but-different","content":"Read the selected paths when you need the multi-select state in surrounding UI.\nRead the focused path or item handle when you need to know where a rename,\nkeyboard action, or command lands next."},"117":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#13","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#keyboard-navigation-follows-the-visible-tree","content":"Keyboard movement works on the visible, expanded tree. Expand or collapse a\nbranch to change which row receives focus next. Search changes the same visible\ntree. So search also changes where keyboard movement lands."},"118":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#14","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#keyboard-navigation-follows-the-visible-tree","content":"For this reason, `solid-trees` exposes focus and selection through the model. It\ndoes not use DOM order or row indexes."},"119":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#15","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#add-search-on-top-of-that-same-state","content":"Search matches the same path-first tree data. It changes what stays visible. But\nit does not add a second identity model. Selection and focus still use paths.\nThe visible window changes around the current query."},"120":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#16","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#guide-default-hide-non-matches","content":"Start with `hide-non-matches`. Change it only when the product needs unrelated\nbranches on screen. This mode fits the common \"find the thing I want\" workflow.\nIt keeps the visible tree close to the current search intent."},"121":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#17","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#guide-default-hide-non-matches","content":"`solid-trees` also supports `collapse-non-matches` and `expand-matches`. Use\nthose modes when the surrounding UI needs more context. But keep\n`hide-non-matches` as the default mental model. The shared meaning of all three\nmodes is in\nShared concepts."},"122":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#18","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#solid-integration-notes","content":"Solid reads from the model through primitives and tracked model getters. Solid\nwrites back through model methods."},"123":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#19","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#solid-integration-notes","content":"Use `createFileTreeSelector(...)` when sibling UI needs a custom derived value.\nOutside Solid scopes, the same getters (`getSelectedPaths()`,\n`getFocusedPath()`, `getSearchMatchingPaths()`) return the current values\nimperatively."},"124":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#20","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#solid-integration-notes","content":"The DOM is not the source of truth. The model is the source of truth."},"125":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#21","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#what-to-read-next","content":"For API lookup, read Solid API or\nModel API."},"126":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#22","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#what-to-read-next","content":"For shared search-mode semantics, read\nShared concepts."},"127":{"id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#23","page_id":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/navigate-selection-focus-and-search#what-to-read-next","content":"For row-level editing workflows that build on this focus model, continue with\nRename, drag, and trigger item actions."},"128":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"page","content":"Rename, drag, and trigger item actions","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions"},"129":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#0","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","content":"Inline rename, drag and drop, and optional context-menu commands, all path-first."},"130":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#1","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#start-with-direct-row-actions","content":"Start with direct row actions"},"131":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#2","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#rename-items-in-place","content":"Rename items in place"},"132":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#3","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#move-items-with-drag-and-drop","content":"Move items with drag and drop"},"133":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#4","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#combine-rename-and-drag-safely","content":"Combine rename and drag safely"},"134":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#5","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#add-a-context-menu-as-an-optional-command-surface","content":"Add a context menu as an optional command surface"},"135":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#6","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#lock-window-scroll-while-a-menu-is-open","content":"Lock window scroll while a menu is open"},"136":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#7","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#keep-keyboard-and-focus-behavior-predictable","content":"Keep keyboard and focus behavior predictable"},"137":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#8","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#the-tree-owns-the-interaction-surface-your-app-owns-persistence","content":"The tree owns the interaction surface. Your app owns persistence."},"138":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#9","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","content":"This guide covers the main row-level editing workflows. Rename comes first. Drag\nand drop comes second. Optional command surfaces, such as context menus, come\nlast. Every callback stays path-first."},"139":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#10","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#start-with-direct-row-actions","content":"The core row workflows are:"},"140":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#11","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#start-with-direct-row-actions","content":"rename in place"},"141":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#12","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#start-with-direct-row-actions","content":"drag and drop"},"142":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#13","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#start-with-direct-row-actions","content":"optional context-menu commands"},"143":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#14","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#start-with-direct-row-actions","content":"Learn these flows before you use a generic mutation inventory. Users care about\nthree things. They care about which path moved. They care about which path\nchanged its name. They care about which row stays focused next."},"144":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#15","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#rename-items-in-place","content":"Enable `renaming` when users must rename rows inline. The common policy hooks\nare:"},"145":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#16","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#rename-items-in-place","content":"`canRename(item)` to block protected files or folders"},"146":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#17","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#rename-items-in-place","content":"`onRename(event)` to respond to the final path change"},"147":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#18","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#rename-items-in-place","content":"`onError(error)` to show invalid rename attempts"},"148":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#19","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#rename-items-in-place","content":"The rename event reports three values. It reports the source path. It reports\nthe destination path. It reports whether the item was a folder."},"149":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#20","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#move-items-with-drag-and-drop","content":"Enable `dragAndDrop` when users must move files or folders directly in the tree.\nThe practical hooks are:"},"150":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#21","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#move-items-with-drag-and-drop","content":"`canDrag(paths)` to lock specific paths"},"151":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#22","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#move-items-with-drag-and-drop","content":"`canDrop(event)` to reject invalid destinations"},"152":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#23","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#move-items-with-drag-and-drop","content":"`onDropComplete(event)` for persistence or adjacent UI updates"},"153":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#24","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#move-items-with-drag-and-drop","content":"`onDropError(error, event)` for visible failures"},"154":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#25","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#move-items-with-drag-and-drop","content":"Drop callbacks report the dragged paths and the resolved target shape. They do\nnot ask you to find the DOM rows yourself."},"155":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#26","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#combine-rename-and-drag-safely","content":"A common editable project tree enables both `renaming` and `dragAndDrop`. Then\nit adds policy guards for protected paths."},"156":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#27","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#combine-rename-and-drag-safely","content":"Typical rules include:"},"157":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#28","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#combine-rename-and-drag-safely","content":"block rename for root config files such as `package.json`"},"158":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#29","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#combine-rename-and-drag-safely","content":"reject drops into generated directories such as `dist/`"},"159":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#30","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#combine-rename-and-drag-safely","content":"show rename and drop failures outside the tree; do not fail silently"},"160":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#31","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#add-a-context-menu-as-an-optional-command-surface","content":"Context menus are secondary command surfaces over the same model. Keep rename\nand drag available. Do not require the menu."},"161":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#32","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#add-a-context-menu-as-an-optional-command-surface","content":"Pass `renderContextMenu` to `<FileTree>` and the tree renders the menu content\nfor you, as Solid JSX. Providing it enables the context menu.\n`composition.contextMenu` keeps the behavior flags:"},"162":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#33","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#add-a-context-menu-as-an-optional-command-surface","content":"The menu renders inside the tree's floating anchor. If you portal menu content\nelsewhere, mark its root with `data-file-tree-context-menu-root=\"true\"` so that\nclicks inside it do not count as outside clicks."},"163":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#34","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#lock-window-scroll-while-a-menu-is-open","content":"A user scroll inside the tree pane closes the open menu. But the tree cannot\nreach the scroll containers that your page owns. A menu opened with a right\nclick sits at a fixed point in the viewport, and so does a portaled menu. So the\nmenu disconnects from its row when the window scrolls. Lock the window scroll\nfor the menu's lifetime. Release the lock when the menu closes:"},"164":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#35","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#lock-window-scroll-while-a-menu-is-open","content":"Some menu primitives have a modal mode that locks scroll for you. Apply the\nmanual lock for a non-modal menu."},"165":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#36","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#keep-keyboard-and-focus-behavior-predictable","content":"The focused row is the anchor for rename and keyboard commands. Restore focus in\na predictable way when a menu opens and closes. Keyboard users must reach the\nmain actions without pointer-only gestures."},"166":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#37","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#the-tree-owns-the-interaction-surface-your-app-owns-persistence","content":"`solid-trees` emits path-based rename and drop events. Your app decides whether\nto save those changes. It can save them to a server, local state, or another\nboundary. Keep this separation clear."},"167":{"id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#38","page_id":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/rename-drag-and-trigger-item-actions#the-tree-owns-the-interaction-surface-your-app-owns-persistence","content":"To learn the shared terms behind those events, read\nShared concepts. For API\nlookup, read Solid API or\nModel API."},"168":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","type":"page","content":"Shape tree data for fast rendering","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering"},"169":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#0","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","content":"Prepare tree input outside the UI so the client skips repeated shape and sort work."},"170":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#1","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#start-with-server-prepared-input-for-scalable-trees","content":"Start with server-prepared input for scalable trees"},"171":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#2","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#pass-preparedinput-into-the-model","content":"Pass `preparedInput` into the model"},"172":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#3","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#use-simple-paths-input-only-for-small-trees","content":"Use simple `paths` input only for small trees"},"173":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#4","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#presorted-input-is-the-highest-performance-prepared-input-path","content":"Presorted input is the highest-performance prepared-input path"},"174":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#5","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#prepare-on-the-server-render-on-the-client","content":"Prepare on the server, render on the client"},"175":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#6","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#keep-client-side-sorting-and-preparation-secondary","content":"Keep client-side sorting and preparation secondary"},"176":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#7","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","content":"Shape the tree before it reaches the UI when the dataset is large. Then this\nout-of-UI preparation is worth the effort. `solid-trees` treats prepared input\nas a first-class public concept. It is not an internal optimization trick."},"177":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#8","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#start-with-server-prepared-input-for-scalable-trees","content":"`prepareFileTreeInput(...)` lets the client skip repeated tree-shape work. Do\nthis preparation on the server, a loader, or another non-UI boundary. That\nboundary already has the full path list."},"178":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#9","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#start-with-server-prepared-input-for-scalable-trees","content":"The client still uses the same path-first model. Only the expensive preparation\nstep moves earlier."},"179":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#10","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#pass-preparedinput-into-the-model","content":"`createFileTree(...)` accepts the prepared payload in place of raw `paths`."},"180":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#11","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#use-simple-paths-input-only-for-small-trees","content":"Raw `paths` is still the right choice for small demos, tests, and very small\nstatic trees."},"181":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#12","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#use-simple-paths-input-only-for-small-trees","content":"This is the easy start path, not the scale-oriented default. Move the\npreparation work out of the client when the tree grows."},"182":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#13","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#presorted-input-is-the-highest-performance-prepared-input-path","content":"Use `preparePresortedFileTreeInput(...)` when your server or indexer already\nknows the final order. It skips the tree-shape work. It also skips the extra\nsort work that a normal prepared-input pass applies."},"183":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#14","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#presorted-input-is-the-highest-performance-prepared-input-path","content":"Use this path when the backend, build step, or cached index already owns the\nsort order. Do not write the default order again in the client only to reach the\npresorted path."},"184":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#15","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#prepare-on-the-server-render-on-the-client","content":"Use this simple split:"},"185":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#16","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#prepare-on-the-server-render-on-the-client","content":"Load canonical paths outside the UI."},"186":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#17","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#prepare-on-the-server-render-on-the-client","content":"Prepare the input one time."},"187":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#18","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#prepare-on-the-server-render-on-the-client","content":"Pass `preparedInput` into `createFileTree(...)`, or into `resetPaths(...)`\nwhen the tree already exists."},"188":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#19","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#prepare-on-the-server-render-on-the-client","content":"This split reduces client CPU work. It makes the startup cost more predictable.\nIt also lets one prepared payload feed every tree that shows the same paths."},"189":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#20","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#keep-client-side-sorting-and-preparation-secondary","content":"Sometimes the data exists only in the browser. Sometimes custom order must run\nthere. `solid-trees` supports both cases, but they are the exception path. Start\nwith prepared input when the app can move the work out of the UI. Use\nclient-side shaping only as a fallback."},"190":{"id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#21","page_id":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/shape-tree-data-for-fast-rendering#keep-client-side-sorting-and-preparation-secondary","content":"To learn the shared contract behind these inputs, read\nShared concepts. For the scale-oriented\nversion of this guidance, continue with\nHandle large trees efficiently."},"191":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","type":"page","content":"Show Git status and row annotations","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations"},"192":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#0","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","content":"Built-in Git status signals and custom row decorations for everything else."},"193":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#1","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"Start with built-in `gitStatus`"},"194":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#2","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#know-when-gitstatus-is-enough","content":"Know when `gitStatus` is enough"},"195":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#3","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#update-status-sets-over-time","content":"Update status sets over time"},"196":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#4","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#add-custom-row-annotations-with-renderrowdecoration","content":"Add custom row annotations with `renderRowDecoration`"},"197":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#5","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#choose-between-the-two-paths","content":"Choose between the two paths"},"198":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#6","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#keep-styling-separate-from-annotation-meaning","content":"Keep styling separate from annotation meaning"},"199":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#7","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","content":"Use built-in `gitStatus` when the signal is Git-like. Use `renderRowDecoration`\nwhen the row needs product-specific metadata that is not Git state."},"200":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#8","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"`gitStatus` is the default row-signal path. It attaches statuses to canonical\npaths. Folders can reflect changed descendants automatically."},"201":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#9","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"`solid-trees` supports these built-in statuses:"},"202":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#10","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"`added`"},"203":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#11","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"`modified`"},"204":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#12","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"`deleted`"},"205":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#13","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"`ignored`"},"206":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#14","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"`renamed`"},"207":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#15","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"`untracked`"},"208":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#16","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#start-with-built-in-gitstatus","content":"This is the shortest path when the row signal already matches Git semantics."},"209":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#17","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#know-when-gitstatus-is-enough","content":"Use `gitStatus` only when the meaning is Git-like. Do not invent fake Git state\nonly to show a badge. Let `solid-trees` own the built-in status lane. Let the\nstyling system control how those signals look."},"210":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#18","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#update-status-sets-over-time","content":"Replace the current status data directly when the surrounding app changes.\nExamples are new commits, branches, comparison views, or status visibility. Use\n`applyGitStatusPatch(...)` when only a few paths change."},"211":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#19","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#update-status-sets-over-time","content":"This step keeps the runtime accurate. It does not promise a filesystem watcher\nor a repo-sync framework."},"212":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#20","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#add-custom-row-annotations-with-renderrowdecoration","content":"Use `renderRowDecoration` when the row needs metadata that is not Git-like."},"213":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#21","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#add-custom-row-annotations-with-renderrowdecoration","content":"Good fits include generated-file markers, remote-storage indicators, validation\nmarkers, and short secondary labels."},"214":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#22","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#choose-between-the-two-paths","content":"Use:"},"215":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#23","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#choose-between-the-two-paths","content":"`gitStatus` when the meaning is Git-like"},"216":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#24","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#choose-between-the-two-paths","content":"`renderRowDecoration` for everything else"},"217":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#25","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#choose-between-the-two-paths","content":"both together when a row needs Git state and one extra product-specific signal"},"218":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#26","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#choose-between-the-two-paths","content":"Keep annotations short and easy to read. Give a decoration accessible text or a\ntooltip when it affects user decisions."},"219":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#27","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#keep-styling-separate-from-annotation-meaning","content":"Keep Git-status colors and decoration visuals in the same appearance system as\nthe rest of the tree. Use the styling and theming controls for color. Do not add\na second theme layer for annotations."},"220":{"id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#28","page_id":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/show-git-status-and-row-annotations#keep-styling-separate-from-annotation-meaning","content":"For that part of the API, read\nStyle and theme the tree and\nStyling and theming."},"221":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","type":"page","content":"Style and theme the tree","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree"},"222":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#0","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","content":"Host styles, CSS variables, editor themes, density, and plain page CSS."},"223":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#1","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#start-with-host-styling","content":"Start with host styling"},"224":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#2","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#use-css-variables-for-most-visual-changes","content":"Use CSS variables for most visual changes"},"225":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#3","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#match-an-editor-palette-with-themetotreestyles","content":"Match an editor palette with `themeToTreeStyles(...)`"},"226":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#4","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#set-density-with-density","content":"Set density with `density`"},"227":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#5","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#follow-your-pages-color-scheme","content":"Follow your page's color scheme"},"228":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#6","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#choose-the-right-styling-layer","content":"Choose the right styling layer"},"229":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#7","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#page-css-is-the-escape-hatch","content":"Page CSS is the escape hatch"},"230":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#8","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","content":"Start with the host element. Then move inward. Host styles control the outer\npanel. CSS variables control most of the tree appearance.\n`themeToTreeStyles(...)` maps an editor-like theme into the same variable\nsystem. The tree renders into the light DOM, so plain page CSS is the escape\nhatch when the supported surfaces cannot reach a narrow case."},"231":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#9","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#start-with-host-styling","content":"The host element is the outer panel boundary. Use it for width, height, borders,\nradius, background, and layout placement."},"232":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#10","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#start-with-host-styling","content":"Pass normal host props to `<FileTree model={...} />`."},"233":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#11","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#use-css-variables-for-most-visual-changes","content":"CSS variables are the main public styling surface inside the tree. Use them\nbefore you write selectors against the tree's markup. Solid's `style` object\ntakes custom properties directly."},"234":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#12","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#use-css-variables-for-most-visual-changes","content":"You can also set the variables on any ancestor in a stylesheet. This approach\nkeeps the normal fallback chain in place:"},"235":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#13","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#use-css-variables-for-most-visual-changes","content":"explicit override variables (`--trees-*-override`)"},"236":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#14","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#use-css-variables-for-most-visual-changes","content":"`--trees-theme-*` variables from theme helpers"},"237":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#15","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#use-css-variables-for-most-visual-changes","content":"library defaults"},"238":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#16","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#use-css-variables-for-most-visual-changes","content":"For the token families behind those variables, read\nStyling and theming."},"239":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#17","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#match-an-editor-palette-with-themetotreestyles","content":"Use `themeToTreeStyles(...)` when your app already has a VS Code or Shiki-style\ntheme object. It maps that theme to host styles. It also maps the theme to the\n`--trees-theme-*` variables that the tree understands. The result is a Solid\n`style` object, keyed by CSS property names."},"240":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#18","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#match-an-editor-palette-with-themetotreestyles","content":"This helper gives you matching panel colors, selection colors, search-field\ncolors, and Git-status colors. You do not rebuild the theme system by hand."},"241":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#19","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#set-density-with-density","content":"Pass `density` to `createFileTree(...)`. This option sets row height and spacing\ntogether. The keyword form (`'compact'`, `'default'`, `'relaxed'`) sets both\nvalues at once. The numeric form keeps the default row height and sets a custom\nspacing factor. `<FileTree>` paints `--trees-item-height` and\n`--trees-density-override` onto the host from the resolved density. This step\nkeeps the virtualized row height and the painted row height aligned. Set\n`itemHeight` only when you need a row height that does not match a preset."},"242":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#20","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#set-density-with-density","content":"The keyword presets are exported as `FILE_TREE_DENSITY_PRESETS`. So helpers such\nas `initialVisibleRowCount` can divide by the preset row height. They do not\nhard-code it."},"243":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#21","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#follow-your-pages-color-scheme","content":"The tree root sets `color-scheme: light dark`, so its colors follow the OS\npreference. When your page has its own light and dark toggle, pin the tree to it\nwith `style={{ 'color-scheme': 'dark' }}`, `'inherit'`, or a page CSS rule."},"244":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#22","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#choose-the-right-styling-layer","content":"Use:"},"245":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#23","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#choose-the-right-styling-layer","content":"host styles for layout and panel framing"},"246":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#24","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#choose-the-right-styling-layer","content":"CSS variables for product-specific appearance changes inside the tree"},"247":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#25","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#choose-the-right-styling-layer","content":"`themeToTreeStyles(...)` when the tree must inherit an editor palette"},"248":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#26","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#choose-the-right-styling-layer","content":"plain page CSS for narrow cases the variables cannot express"},"249":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#27","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#choose-the-right-styling-layer","content":"Add explicit overrides on top when the imported theme is close but not final."},"250":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#28","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#page-css-is-the-escape-hatch","content":"Upstream injects custom CSS into its shadow root through `unsafeCSS`. Here the\ntree lives in the light DOM, so ordinary page CSS reaches it. Unlayered page CSS\noverrides every `solid-trees` layer. Keep it small, local, and secondary."},"251":{"id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#29","page_id":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/style-and-theme-the-tree#page-css-is-the-escape-hatch","content":"Do not start here. Do not rebuild the whole visual system from raw selectors.\nFor a broad lookup of the supported styling surfaces, read\nStyling and theming. For icon-specific\nappearance changes, continue with\nCustomize icons."},"252":{"id":"/docs","page_id":"/docs","type":"page","content":"Overview","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs"},"253":{"id":"/docs#0","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Guide-first documentation for solid-trees, covering the Solid API, prepared input, styling, icons, Git status, and large trees."},"254":{"id":"/docs#1","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"**solid-trees** uses one path-first model. It is a SolidJS 2 port of\n`@pierre/trees`: the model layer is vendored from\nupstream, and the view is a native Solid rewrite rendered into the light DOM.\nSelection, focus, search, rename, drag and drop, Git status, and row annotations\nall use canonical paths."},"255":{"id":"/docs#2","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"<OverviewTree />"},"256":{"id":"/docs#3","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"These docs are guide-first. First, create a model and render it. Next, shape the\ntree data before it reaches the UI. Then add search, item actions, styling,\nicons, or row signals when you need them."},"257":{"id":"/docs#4","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Create a model with `createFileTree` and render it with `<FileTree>`."},"258":{"id":"/docs#5","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"The path-first vocabulary behind every option and event."},"259":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","type":"page","content":"Differences from @pierre/trees","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees"},"260":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#0","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","content":"What changed in the SolidJS 2 port, and what did not."},"261":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#1","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#runtimes","content":"Runtimes"},"262":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#2","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#dom-and-styling","content":"DOM and styling"},"263":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#3","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#composition","content":"Composition"},"264":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#4","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#icons","content":"Icons"},"265":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#5","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","content":"`solid-trees` vendors upstream's model layer (path store, controller, focus,\nselection, search, rename, drag and drop, Git status). The view is a native\nSolid 2 rewrite of upstream's Preact `FileTreeView`, rendered into the light\nDOM, with upstream's visual styles shipped as an importable stylesheet."},"266":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#6","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#runtimes","content":"One runtime: `createFileTree(...)` plus `<FileTree>`. There is no React\nwrapper, vanilla `FileTree` class, `render(...)`, or `hydrate(...)`."},"267":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#7","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#runtimes","content":"No SSR or hydration: no `preloadFileTree(...)`, `@pierre/trees/ssr` entry, or\n`preloadedData` prop. The tree renders on the client."},"268":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#8","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#dom-and-styling","content":"No shadow DOM, custom element, or `unsafeCSS`; use page CSS."},"269":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#9","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#dom-and-styling","content":"The DOM keeps upstream's data attributes (`data-type=\"item\"`,\n`data-item-path`, `data-item-selected`, `data-item-focused`,\n`data-item-git-status`, `data-item-section`, `data-file-tree-sticky-row`, ...)\nand name markup (`MiddleTruncate` / `Truncate`), so upstream CSS ports over."},"270":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#10","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#dom-and-styling","content":"`themeToTreeStyles` returns CSS property names (`background-color`), not\nReact's camelCase."},"271":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#11","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#dom-and-styling","content":"The scrollbar gutter (`--trees-scrollbar-gutter-measured`) is measured once\nper document, not per tree."},"272":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#12","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#composition","content":"Header and context-menu content are Solid JSX (`header`, `renderContextMenu`\nprops); `composition.contextMenu` keeps the behavior flags (`enabled`,\n`triggerMode`, `buttonVisibility`) and `onOpen`/`onClose`."},"273":{"id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#13","page_id":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/differences-from-pierre-trees#icons","content":"Each tree prefixes the symbol ids of its sprite sheet (built-in set plus\n`icons.spriteSheet`) so several trees in one document never resolve each\nother's icons; names a sheet does not define resolve document-wide."},"274":{"id":"/docs/reference/icons","page_id":"/docs/reference/icons","type":"page","content":"Icons","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/icons"},"275":{"id":"/docs/reference/icons#0","page_id":"/docs/reference/icons","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/icons","content":"Lookup reference for icon sets, remaps, sprite sheets, and resolution order."},"276":{"id":"/docs/reference/icons#1","page_id":"/docs/reference/ico