nent renders the model into the light DOM."],[602,"The stylesheet uses three cascade layers:\n`@layer solid-trees.structure, solid-trees.base, solid-trees.theme`."],[383,"The traversal methods follow the current visible tree, including its active\nexpansion and search state. `getVisibleRows(start, end)` uses inclusive row\nindexes and returns path-first row metadata for building custom navigation."],[112,"The tree model tracks three user-facing states: selection, focus, and the\nvisible row set. Search builds on the same model. It does not add a separate\nidentity system."],[137,"The tree owns the interaction surface. Your app owns persistence."],[609,"The tree renders into the light DOM, so page CSS reaches it. It replaces\nupstream's `unsafeCSS`. Use it only when the supported host and variable\nsurfaces cannot express the customization."],[243,"The tree root sets `color-scheme: light dark`, so its colors follow the OS\npreference. When your page has its own light and dark toggle, pin the tree to it\nwith `style={{ 'color-scheme': 'dark' }}`, `'inherit'`, or a page CSS rule."],[580,"The tree styling surface uses variable families, not one flat list."],[466,"The tree-shape options are `paths`, `preparedInput`, `initialExpansion`,\n`initialExpandedPaths`, `flattenEmptyDirectories`, and `sort`."],[256,"These docs are guide-first. First, create a model and render it. Next, shape the\ntree data before it reaches the UI. Then add search, item actions, styling,\nicons, or row signals when you need them."],[359,"These reads stay path-oriented and model-oriented. They do not ask you to infer\nthe tree state from DOM order. Inside Solid scopes they track, so JSX and memos\nupdate when the model changes."],[29,"This approach keeps the built-in mapping for every icon that you did not change."],[138,"This guide covers the main row-level editing workflows. Rename comes first. Drag\nand drop comes second. Optional command surfaces, such as context menus, come\nlast. Every callback stays path-first."],[240,"This helper gives you matching panel colors, selection colors, search-field\ncolors, and Git-status colors. You do not rebuild the theme system by hand."],[93,"This is another reason to start with the input story first. Do not recompute the\ntree shape in the client while the visible tree also changes often."],[181,"This is the easy start path, not the scale-oriented default. Move the\npreparation work out of the client when the tree grows."],[208,"This is the shortest path when the row signal already matches Git semantics."],[81,"This order matters. Do not go straight to the rendering knobs while the client\nstill does avoidable shape or sort work."],[576,"This page is the lookup reference for host styling, CSS-variable families,\n`themeToTreeStyles(...)`, the stylesheet layers, and page CSS."],[284,"This page is the lookup reference for icon sets, icon remaps, sprite-sheet\nextension, and icon-specific configuration rules."],[412,"This page names the runtime touchpoints such as `setGitStatus(...)` and\n`setIcons(...)`. For styling variables and theme helpers, read\nStyling and theming. For icon remaps and\nicon-set lookup, read Icons."],[425,"This page owns the shared language for `solid-trees`. Every option, primitive,\nand model method uses the same path-first identity model. They use the same\ntree-defining inputs. They use the same mutation vocabulary."],[405,"This separation keeps model subscriptions for state reads. It keeps mutation\nevents for semantic side effects."],[188,"This split reduces client CPU work. It makes the startup cost more predictable.\nIt also lets one prepared payload feed every tree that shows the same paths."],[211,"This step keeps the runtime accurate. It does not promise a filesystem watcher\nor a repo-sync framework."],[439,"This table lists the meaning of the `createFileTree(...)` options."],[190,"To learn the shared contract behind these inputs, read\nShared concepts. For the scale-oriented\nversion of this guidance, continue with\nHandle large trees efficiently."],[50,"To learn the shared terms behind this rule, read\nShared concepts."],[167,"To learn the shared terms behind those events, read\nShared concepts. For API\nlookup, read Solid API or\nModel API."],[403,"To localize the text the tree renders (the search placeholder, accessible names,\nand git status tooltips), pass a `labels` option at construction or call\n`setLabels(...)` later. Any label you leave out falls back to its English\ndefault from `DEFAULT_FILE_TREE_LABELS`."],[76,"To make large trees fast, first reduce unnecessary client work. Shape and order\nthe data before it reaches the UI. Then tune rendering only where the visible\nwindow needs help."],[418,"Tree-shape options"],[322,"Turn off the built-in colored icons with `colored: false`. The token lookup and\nthe fallback precedence for icon colors are in\nStyling and theming."],[156,"Typical rules include:"],[551,"Typical write paths are:"],[606,"Unlayered page CSS overrides every layer."],[66,"Unlike upstream, `solid-trees` renders on the client only. There is no SSR\npreload or hydration step. See\nDifferences from `@pierre/trees`."],[320,"update it later with `setIcons(...)`"],[195,"Update status sets over time"],[250,"Upstream injects custom CSS into its shadow root through `unsafeCSS`. Here the\ntree lives in the light DOM, so ordinary page CSS reaches it. Unlayered page CSS\noverrides every `solid-trees` layer. Keep it small, local, and secondary."],[562,"Use `composition.contextMenu` on the model for the behavior flags (`enabled`,\n`triggerMode`, `buttonVisibility`) and the `onOpen` / `onClose` callbacks. The\nshared meaning of the composition options is in\nShared concepts."],[123,"Use `createFileTreeSelector(...)` when sibling UI needs a custom derived value.\nOutside Solid scopes, the same getters (`getSelectedPaths()`,\n`getFocusedPath()`, `getSearchMatchingPaths()`) return the current values\nimperatively."],[59,"Use `createFileTreeSelector(model, selector, isEqual?)` when sibling UI needs a\ncustom derived value. It only notifies when the selected value changes. Model\ngetters such as `getFocusedPath()` also track inside Solid scopes, so you can\nread them directly in JSX. For the shared interaction terms, read\nNavigate selection, focus, and search\nand Solid API."],[291,"use `FileTreeIconConfig` when a string set is not enough"],[209,"Use `gitStatus` only when the meaning is Git-like. Do not invent fake Git state\nonly to show a badge. Let `solid-trees` own the built-in status lane. Let the\nstyling system control how those signals look."],[438,"Use `prepareFileTreeInput(...)` and `preparePresortedFileTreeInput(...)` from\n`solid-trees` to create the prepared forms. Treat prepared input as an opaque\ntree-input object. Do not build the shape by hand."],[83,"Use `prepareFileTreeInput(...)` instead when you have only an unsorted path\nlist. In both cases, pass the prepared result into `createFileTree(...)` or\n`resetPaths(...)`."],[65,"Use `preparePresortedFileTreeInput(...)` when the server already knows the final\norder. It is the highest-performance prepared-input variant. The client can skip\nboth the shaping work and the extra sorting work."],[182,"Use `preparePresortedFileTreeInput(...)` when your server or indexer already\nknows the final order. It skips the tree-shape work. It also skips the extra\nsort work that a normal prepared-input pass applies."],[212,"Use `renderRowDecoration` when the row needs metadata that is not Git-like."],[37,"Use `spriteSheet` in two cases. Use it when you already have branded SVG\nsymbols. Also use it when you need a few custom symbols next to the built-in\nset."],[404,"Use `subscribe(listener)` when code outside Solid must refresh a derived\nsnapshot on any tree change. Use `onMutation(...)` when add, remove, move,\nreset, or batch intent matters. Both return an unsubscribe function."],[239,"Use `themeToTreeStyles(...)` when your app already has a VS Code or Shiki-style\ntheme object. It maps that theme to host styles. It also maps the theme to the\n`--trees-theme-*` variables that the tree understands. The result is a Solid\n`style` object, keyed by CSS property names."],[15,"Use a sprite sheet only for advanced cases"],[199,"Use built-in `gitStatus` when the signal is Git-like. Use `renderRowDecoration`\nwhen the row needs product-specific metadata that is not Git state."],[49,"Use canonical path strings as the public identity for each item in the tree. For\nexample, `src/components/Button.tsx` is more than a label on screen. It is the\nvalue that you read from selection state. It is the path that you focus in code.\nIt is also the target that you rename or move later."],[224,"Use CSS variables for most visual changes"],[522,"Use model methods when the tree changes after mount. The methods include\n`resetPaths(...)`, `setComposition(...)`, `setGitStatus(...)`, `setIcons(...)`,\n`setLabels(...)`, and the search or mutation methods."],[563,"Use primitives and tracked getters when Solid UI needs reactive reads. Use\n`model.onMutation(...)` when you need semantic side effects. Examples are\npersistence, logging, or analytics around add, remove, move, reset, or batch\noperations."],[172,"Use simple `paths` input only for small trees"],[46,"Use simple `paths` input only when the tree is small"],[13,"Use the object form for targeted remaps"],[317,"Use the object form when the replacement symbol needs non-default geometry\nmetadata."],[23,"Use the styling system for broader appearance control. Do not treat icons as a\nparallel theme surface. Read\nStyle and theme the tree."],[467,"Use them to answer three questions:"],[502,"Use these events to sync adjacent UI, analytics, or persistence layers. Do not\nturn them into a filesystem-sync tutorial."],[183,"Use this path when the backend, build step, or cached index already owns the\nsort order. Do not write the default order again in the client only to reach the\npresorted path."],[61,"Use this recommended scale path:"],[184,"Use this simple split:"],[77,"Use:"],[214,"Use:"],[244,"Use:"],[554,"visible row inspection through `getFocusedIndex()`, `getVisibleCount()`, and\n`getVisibleRows(start, end)` when surrounding UI needs custom navigation."],[553,"visible-tree traversal through `focusFirstItem()`, `focusLastItem()`,\n`focusNextItem()`, `focusPreviousItem()`, and `focusParentItem()`."],[260,"What changed in the SolidJS 2 port, and what did not."],[469,"what the initial visible shape must be"],[75,"What to read next"],[111,"What to read next"],[94,"When the path list changes wholesale, keep the model and call `resetPaths(...)`.\nPass `{ preparedInput }` alone to skip the parse and sort that raw `paths` would\nrequire. Swapping the `model` prop of `<FileTree>` remounts the whole view."],[468,"where the tree data comes from"],[470,"whether the client must still shape or sort the data"],[331,"Write and control APIs"],[510,"Writing to the model from Solid"],[234,"You can also set the variables on any ancestor in a stylesheet. This approach\nkeeps the normal fallback chain in place:"],[97,"you re-sort on the client after the server already chose the order"],[99,"you rebuild the model when `resetPaths(...)` with matching prepared input does\nthe job"],[96,"you send only raw `paths` for huge server-known datasets"],[98,"you use low-level virtualization ideas as the first fix"]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":7,"2":8,"3":9,"4":10,"5":11,"6":12,"7":13,"8":14,"9":15,"10":16,"11":17,"12":18,"13":19,"14":20,"15":21,"16":22,"17":23,"18":24,"19":25,"20":26,"21":27,"22":28,"23":29,"24":30,"25":31,"26":32,"27":33,"28":34,"29":35,"30":36,"31":37,"32":38,"33":39,"34":40,"35":41,"36":42,"37":43,"38":44,"39":45,"40":46,"41":47,"42":48,"43":49,"44":50,"45":51,"46":52,"47":53,"48":54,"49":55,"50":56,"51":57,"52":58,"53":59,"54":60,"55":61,"56":62,"57":63,"58":64,"59":65,"60":66,"61":67,"62":68,"63":69,"64":70,"65":71,"66":72,"67":73,"68":74,"6